iplinary agreement** confirms that **(A) aldosterone excess (primary hyperaldosteronism)** is the **most likely etiology** of this patient’s **resistant hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis**. Here’s the **unified action plan**:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Diagnostic Confirmation:**\n- **Primary aldosterone testing**:\n  - **Plasma aldosterone concentration (PAC) + plasma renin activity (PRA)** → **PAC/PRA ratio > 20-30** (with PAC > 15 ng/dL) is diagnostic for primary hyperaldosteronism.\n  - **If ratio is indeterminate**, proceed with:\n    - **Saline infusion test** (gold standard) or\n    - **Fludrocortisone suppression test**.\n\n- **Adrenal imaging**:\n  - **CT/MRI of the adrenal glands** to differentiate:\n    - **Aldosterone-producing adenoma (APA)** vs.\n    - **Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia (BAH)**.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Medication Adjustments:**\n- **Discontinue hydrochlorothiazide (HCTZ)** → Contributes to hypokalemia and may mask aldosterone excess.\n- **Replace with potassium-sparing diuretic if needed** (e.g., amiloride or triamterene) **only if renal function permits**.\n- **Consider spironolactone (aldosterone antagonist)** if primary hyperaldosteronism is confirmed:\n  - **Dose**: Start at **12.5–25 mg/day**, titrate to BP control (max 50 mg/day).\n  - **Monitor potassium closely** (risk of hyperkalemia, especially with lisinopril).\n- **Continue amlodipine** (CCB) for vascular effects, but **BP control may still require additional agents** (e.g., calcium channel blockers or beta-blockers if needed).\n\n---\n\n### **3. Cardiovascular Monitoring:**\n- **ECG**:\n  - Assess for **hypokalemia-induced arrhythmias** (e.g., U waves, prolonged QT, atrial fibrillation).\n  - **Correct hypokalemia aggressively** (oral potassium supplements or IV if severe).\n- **Echocardiogram**:\n  - Evaluate for **left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)** or **diastolic dysfunction** (common in resistant hypertension).\n- **Ambulatory BP monitoring (ABPM)**:\n  - Rule out **m