cy due to elevated ACTH (and MSH) levels stimulating melanocytes.\n\n### Why Not the Other Options?\n- **(E) Order an ACTH stimulation test** – While this is the definitive diagnostic test for adrenal insufficiency, the patient’s presentation is **highly suggestive of an acute adrenal crisis** (hypotension, weight loss, abdominal pain). **Delaying treatment to perform a test is dangerous**—this patient needs **immediate glucocorticoid replacement**.\n- **(C) Start him on androgen replacement treatment** – Androgen deficiency (e.g., DHEA) is not an immediate concern in acute adrenal crisis.\n- **(B) Administer intravenous fludrocortisone** – Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid, but in acute adrenal crisis, **hydrocortisone (which has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity at high doses) is the priority**.\n- **(D) Start him on both hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone therapy** – While this is the **long-term treatment** for primary adrenal insufficiency, in the **acute setting**, **intravenous hydrocortisone alone is sufficient** (fludrocortisone can be added later if needed).\n\n### **Next Best Step: (A) Administer intravenous hydrocortisone**\n- **Immediate IV hydrocortisone** (100 mg bolus, followed by continuous infusion or divided doses) is the **cornerstone of treatment for adrenal crisis**.\n- After stabilization, an **ACTH stimulation test** can confirm the diagnosis, and **fludrocortisone** can be added for long-term mineralocorticoid replacement.\n\n### **Additional Immediate Steps:**\n- **IV fluids (normal saline)** to correct hypotension and volume depletion.\n- **Monitor electrolytes** (especially sodium, potassium, and glucose).\n- **Check cortisol and ACTH levels** (before hydrocortisone administration if possible).\n\nWould you like further details on long-term management or diagnostic confirmation?', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant'), UserMessage(content='Given the opinions from other medical agents, indicate whether y