 **Neuroanatomy of BSS:**\n   - **Ipsilateral (left) corticospinal tract damage** → Left-sided weakness (already present in the patient).\n   - **Contralateral (right) spinothalamic tract damage** → Right-sided loss of pain/temperature sensation (**analgesia**).\n   - **Ipsilateral dorsal column damage** → Left-sided loss of vibration/proprioception (less prominent at C5 but possible).\n\n2. **Why Not the Other Options?**\n   - **(A) Spastic paralysis at the level of lesion** → Incorrect because spasticity develops **below** the lesion, not at the exact level.\n   - **(B) Right-sided Horner’s syndrome** → Incorrect because Horner’s requires **C8-T2 sympathetic chain disruption**, not a C5 lesion.\n   - **(C) Contralateral corticospinal tract involvement** → Incorrect because the corticospinal tract is **ipsilateral** in the spinal cord (crosses in the medulla).\n   - **(E) Contralateral loss of vibration/proprioception** → Incorrect because the dorsal columns are **ipsilateral** (cross in the medulla).\n\n3. **Clinical Relevance:**\n   - **Right-sided analgesia (D)** is the **most reliable and immediate sensory finding** in BSS, whereas spasticity (A) develops later.\n   - The **MRI-confirmed left C5 hemicord lesion** aligns perfectly with this diagnosis.\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe **correct answer is (D) Right-sided analgesia**, as it is the **hallmark contralateral sensory deficit** in Brown-Séquard syndrome.\n\n**No further expert consultation is needed.** This case is a classic presentation of a **traumatic spinal cord hemisection**, and the neuroanatomical principles are well-established. Management should focus on **secondary injury prevention (immobilization, steroids if indicated, and rehabilitation)**.', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant')]
