rarchy of Likelihood**\n1. **(D) Maternal diabetes** – **Best supported by data.**\n2. **(B) Alcohol exposure** – **Possible but less specific.**\n3. **(E) Trisomy 18** – **Unlikely without other features.**\n4. **(C) Trisomy 13** – **Unlikely.**\n5. **(A) Oligohydramnios** – **Irrelevant.**\n\nWould you like to **proceed with additional diagnostic testing** or **focus on counseling the family**?\n', role='user'), AssistantMessage(content='**Final Consensus Opinion (No Further Expert Consultation Needed):**\n\nAfter a thorough **collaborative review** of the clinical presentation, epidemiological data, and phenotypic specificity, the **strongest predisposing factor** for this stillborn infant’s **caudal regression syndrome (CRS)** is:\n\n### **✅ (D) Maternal diabetes**\n\n---\n\n### **Key Supporting Evidence:**\n\n1. **Epidemiological Dominance:**\n   - **16–22% of CRS cases** are linked to **maternal diabetes**, making it the **most common identifiable cause**.\n   - **No prenatal care** increases the likelihood of **undiagnosed diabetes**, which is a significant public health concern.\n\n2. **Phenotypic Specificity:**\n   - The **isolated sacral agenesis, club feet, and pelvic hypoplasia** are **classic features of CRS due to diabetic embryopathy**.\n   - **Alcohol exposure (B)** typically presents with **facial dysmorphisms, growth restriction, or CNS abnormalities**, which are **not described** in this case.\n   - **Trisomy 18 (E)** and **Trisomy 13 (C)** would present with **additional syndromic features** (e.g., clenched fists, rocker-bottom feet, holoprosencephaly), which are **absent here**.\n\n3. **Clinical Context:**\n   - **Diabetes is far more prevalent** (~10% of pregnancies) than **alcohol-related CRS** (~1–2% of FASD cases).\n   - **No other syndromic features** suggest a **genetic cause** over an **environmental one**.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Hierarchy of Likelihood:**\n\n| **Factor**               | **Likelihood** | **Key Reason**                     