nergistic with statins and antiplatelets like aspirin/clopidogrel).\n\n3. **Dosing & Monitoring:**\n   - **Dose:** 100 mg **BID** (start with 50 mg BID if renal/hepatic impairment).\n   - **Monitor:** ABI (Ankle-Brachial Index), symptoms, and side effects (headache, diarrhea, palpitations).\n   - **Avoid in:** Heart failure (NYHA III/IV) due to vasodilatory risks.\n\n4. **Drug Interactions:**\n   - **CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, erythromycin)** → ↑ cilostazol levels (risk of bleeding/vasodilation).\n   - **CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampin, phenytoin)** → ↓ cilostazol efficacy.\n   - **Avoid with:** Other PDE inhibitors (e.g., dipyridamole) due to additive vasodilatory effects.\n\n5. **Alternatives if Cilostazol is Contraindicated:**\n   - **Pentoxifylline** (improves erythrocyte flexibility, weaker evidence than cilostazol).\n   - **Antiplatelets (aspirin/clopidogrel)** for secondary CV prevention.\n   - **Revascularization (angioplasty/stenting)** for severe symptomatic PAD.\n\n6. **Lifestyle Integration:**\n   - **Smoking cessation** (critical for PAD progression).\n   - **Supervised exercise therapy** (most effective non-pharmacologic intervention).\n   - **Diet:** Mediterranean diet + statins to address atherosclerosis.\n\n#### **Patient Counseling Highlights:**\n- **"This medication helps your leg muscles get more blood flow by relaxing blood vessels and preventing clots. Take it as prescribed, even if you feel fine—it works best over time."**\n- **"Avoid grapefruit juice (CYP3A4 interaction) and report dizziness or irregular heartbeat."**\n- **"Walking programs are just as important as the pill—aim for 30–45 minutes daily."**\n\n---\n### **When to Re-evaluate:**\n- **No improvement in 3–6 months:** Consider advanced imaging (CT angiography) or revascularization.\n- **New symptoms (e.g., rest pain, ulcers):** May indicate critical limb ischemia (CLI), requiring urgent intervention.\n\nWould you like a **detailed patient handout** or **protocol for foll