l** for surgical planning if needed.\n\n**If MRI is unavailable or contraindicated:**\n- **Radionuclide scan (D)** is a **reasonable alternative** for early AVN detection.\n- **CT (A)** or **X-ray (C)** are **less sensitive** and should not be first-line.\n\n---\n\n### **Next Steps**\n1. **Order MRI of the hip (non-contrast or contrast if infection is suspected).**\n   - **Key sequences:**\n     - **T1-weighted** (for marrow replacement, e.g., granulomas).\n     - **T2-weighted with fat suppression** (for edema, subchondral fractures).\n     - **STIR or contrast-enhanced** (if infection is a concern).\n2. **If MRI is positive for AVN:**\n   - **Stage the disease** (Ficat or Steinberg classification).\n   - **Consult orthopedics** for **core decompression vs. conservative management**.\n3. **If MRI is negative:**\n   - Consider **sarcoid arthritis, bursitis, or referred pain** (e.g., lumbar spine).\n   - **Ultrasound (E)** may help if soft-tissue pathology is suspected.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\nWhile the **orthopedic surgeon’s suggestion of a radionuclide scan** is valid for **very early AVN detection**, **MRI remains the best overall test** due to its **superior specificity, anatomical detail, and ability to evaluate for other pathologies**. Thus, **Option B (MRI of the hip) is the most appropriate choice**.\n\nWould you like guidance on **MRI protocol optimization** for this case?\n(**rheumatologist**): ### **Final Decision: No Further Expert Consultation Needed**\nAfter carefully reviewing the input from the **musculoskeletal radiologist, rheumatologist, and orthopedic surgeon**, I will **refine the recommendation** while maintaining the **MRI as the primary imaging modality** due to its **superior specificity, anatomical detail, and ability to evaluate for multiple pathologies**.\n\n---\n\n### **Consensus Summary & Final Recommendation**\n1. **Primary Diagnosis Suspicion:**\n   - **Avascular Necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head** remains the **most likely diag