h-risk cardiovascular profile (CAD, hypertension, prior stenting)** makes **prolonged hypoxia dangerous**.\n4. **Recent smoking history and altered mental status** increase **aspiration risk**, necessitating **secure airway protection**.\n\n### **Action Plan:**\n1. **Reintubate immediately** (rapid sequence intubation if needed).\n2. **Confirm endotracheal tube placement** (ETCO₂, bilateral breath sounds, CXR).\n3. **Initiate lung-protective ventilation** (tidal volume **6 mL/kg IBW**, PEEP **10-15 cm H₂O**, FiO₂ to maintain SpO₂ > 92%).\n4. **Obtain urgent CXR** (assess for **pulmonary edema, aspiration, or atelectasis**).\n5. **Evaluate for cardiac ischemia** (ECG, troponin, consider echo if unstable).\n6. **Consider bronchoscopy** if **mucus plugging or aspiration** is suspected.\n7. **Optimize volume status** (avoid fluid overload if pulmonary edema is present).\n8. **Empiric antibiotics** if **aspiration pneumonia** is likely.\n\n### **Why No Further Consultation?**\n- The **clinical scenario is straightforward**: **severe hypoxemic respiratory failure post-extubation** in a **high-risk patient**.\n- **All experts agree on the urgency of reintubation**—delay risks **catastrophic outcomes**.\n- **No conflicting opinions** exist; **no additional input is necessary**.\n\n**Proceed with reintubation (Option C) without delay.**\n(**cardiologist**): Given the clinical scenario and the patient’s **rapid deterioration** following extubation—**unresponsiveness, hypoxemia (PaO₂ 73 mm Hg, SpO₂ 73%), and tachypnea (20 breaths/min)**—this is a **critical situation** requiring **immediate intervention**. Here’s the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n### **Key Observations:**\n1. **Post-extubation respiratory failure** (likely due to **postoperative pulmonary edema, aspiration, or residual anesthesia/sedation**).\n2. **Severe hypoxemia (PaO₂ 73 mm Hg, SpO₂ 73%)** despite **FiO₂ 100%** on a **non-rebreather mask**—this indicates **inadequate oxygenation**.\n3. **Cardiovascular risk fa