atient has **additional symptoms** (GI, sexual).\n   - **(E) Conversion disorder:** Typically involves **neurological symptoms** (e.g., paralysis, seizures), which are absent here.\n\n4. **Modern Classification:**\n   - While "somatization disorder" is a DSM-IV term, the **DSM-5 equivalent is Somatic Symptom Disorder**, which better captures the **psychological distress and functional impairment** associated with unexplained symptoms.\n\n#### **Final Recommendations:**\n- **Psychiatric evaluation** to confirm **Somatic Symptom Disorder** and assess for comorbid conditions (e.g., depression, anxiety, trauma).\n- **Collaborative care** with primary care to avoid unnecessary tests while addressing symptoms.\n- **Therapy (CBT, mindfulness-based stress reduction)** to improve coping and reduce symptom-related distress.\n- **Rule out organic causes** (e.g., endometriosis, fibromyalgia, IBS) if symptoms persist or worsen.\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe **most likely diagnosis** is **(C) Somatization disorder**, supported by the patient’s **chronic, multisystem, unexplained symptoms** and the **consensus of the multidisciplinary team**.'}

