vessels**\n  - **Definitive but not initial therapy**—used if bleeding persists after pressure.\n  - **Painful**, requires **local anesthesia**, and can cause **nasal crusting or septal perforation** if overdone.\n  - **Not ideal for a child’s first episode** unless bleeding is severe and uncontrolled.\n\n---\n\n### **Step-by-Step Management Plan:**\n1. **Reassure the patient** (anxiety can worsen bleeding).\n2. **Position the child upright** (head **forward**, not hyperextended).\n3. **Apply firm, continuous pressure** on the **soft part of the nose** (not the bony septum) for **10–15 minutes**.\n4. **Monitor for cessation of bleeding**—if it stops, **no further intervention needed**.\n5. **If bleeding persists after 15 minutes**, consider:\n   - **Oxymetazoline spray** (1–2 sprays per nostril) + **reapply pressure**.\n   - If still bleeding, **anterior nasal packing** (e.g., vaseline gauze) or **cauterization** (if a clear bleeding point is visible).\n6. **Avoid hyperextension** (increases venous pressure and worsens bleeding).\n7. **Discharge instructions:**\n   - Avoid **blowing the nose**, **picking**, or **straining** for **24–48 hours**.\n   - Use **saline nasal spray** to keep mucosa moist.\n   - Follow up if bleeding recurs.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n**(E) Squeezing the nostrils manually for 10 minutes with the head elevated**\nis the **most appropriate initial therapy** for this child’s anterior epistaxis.\n\n---\n### **Collaboration with the ED Team:**\n- **Nurse:** Assist with pressure application and patient comfort.\n- **Pediatrician/ED Physician:** Confirm no underlying coagulopathy (e.g., von Willebrand disease, though unlikely given history).\n- **If pressure fails:** Consider **oxymetazoline spray** or **anterior packing** (with ENT consultation if needed).\n- **Discharge planning:** Educate parents on **avoiding nose-picking**, **humidification**, and **when to return** (e.g., if bleeding persists >30 min after ED visit).\n\nWould you like 