👨‍⚖️  Moderator's final decision: {0.0: '### **Final Decision:**\n\nAfter carefully reviewing the **multidisciplinary team discussion**, the **consensus reasoning**, and the **stepwise clinical approach** provided by the **pediatric emergency medicine specialist, otolaryngologist (ENT), and pediatric hematologist**, the **most appropriate initial therapy** for this **12-year-old boy with spontaneous anterior epistaxis** is:\n\n### **✅ (E) Squeezing the nostrils manually for 10 minutes with the head elevated**\n\n---\n\n### **Key Supporting Points:**\n1. **First-Line, Non-Invasive, and Effective**\n   - Direct compression is the **standard initial management** for anterior epistaxis (the most common type in children).\n   - **~80–90% success rate** when applied correctly (firm pressure on the **soft part of the nose** for **10–15 minutes** with the **head forward**).\n   - Avoids unnecessary interventions (e.g., packing, cautery) that carry risks (pain, infection, scarring).\n\n2. **Patient-Specific Factors**\n   - **No history of coagulopathy, trauma, or recurrent bleeding** → Conservative management is appropriate.\n   - **Stable vital signs** → No need for aggressive measures.\n   - **Child is already applying pressure** → Suggests he can tolerate manual compression.\n\n3. **Stepwise Escalation Plan (If Initial Therapy Fails)**\n   - If bleeding persists after **10–15 minutes of pressure**:\n     1. **Oxymetazoline spray (B)** (vasoconstriction) + reapply pressure.\n     2. **Epinephrine-soaked gauze (C)** (if bleeding is brisk).\n     3. **Silver nitrate cautery (D)** (if a bleeding vessel is visible).\n     4. **Anterior packing (A)** (last resort for refractory cases).\n\n4. **Avoiding Common Pitfalls**\n   - **Hyperextension of the head** (increases venous pressure → worsens bleeding).\n   - **Premature packing or cautery** (unnecessary for a first episode, increases discomfort and complications).\n\n---\n\n### **Why Not the Other Options?**\n| Option | Reason for Exclusion as Initial Therapy |\n|--------|------------------------------------------|\n| **(A) Anterior packing + antibiotics** | Overly aggressive for a first episode; risks include infection, discomfort, and sinusitis. |\n| **(B) Oxymetazoline spray** | Less effective than direct pressure; better as an adjunct if pressure fails. |\n| **(C) Epinephrine gauze** | Useful if pressure fails, but not first-line due to systemic absorption risks (tachycardia, hypertension). |\n| **(D) Silver nitrate cautery** | Painful, requires precise localization of the bleeding vessel, and risks scarring/perforation. |\n\n---\n\n### **Final Recommendation:**\n**Proceed with (E) manual compression for 10 minutes with the head elevated.**\n- If bleeding stops → **discharge with instructions** (avoid nose-picking, humidify air, follow up if recurrence).\n- If bleeding persists → **escalate to oxymetazoline spray, epinephrine gauze, or cautery** as needed.\n\nThis approach aligns with **evidence-based guidelines** (e.g., American Academy of Pediatrics, ENT society recommendations) and the **expert consensus** from the multidisciplinary team.'}

