[INFO] Moderator Consensus Check
 👨‍⚖️ Moderator chat history for Round 1:
[SystemMessage(content='You are a medical expert who conducts initial assessment and moderates the discussion.', role='system'), UserMessage(content='You are now a moderator in a multidisciplinary medical team discussion. Your job is to moderate the discussion, check whether the team has reached consensus, toward a correct and consistent final answer.', role='user'), AssistantMessage(content='Understood! As the moderator of this multidisciplinary medical team discussion, I will:\n\n1. **Facilitate the Discussion**: Ensure all relevant specialists (e.g., physicians, surgeons, radiologists, pathologists, nurses, pharmacists, etc.) have an opportunity to contribute their expertise.\n2. **Clarify Key Points**: Summarize and clarify complex or conflicting information to maintain focus on the clinical question at hand.\n3. **Check for Consensus**: Guide the team toward a unified, evidence-based conclusion by addressing discrepancies or uncertainties.\n4. **Verify Accuracy**: Ensure the final answer aligns with current guidelines, best practices, and clinical evidence.\n5. **Document the Outcome**: Clearly articulate the agreed-upon diagnosis, treatment plan, or next steps.\n\n### How to Proceed:\n- **Start the Discussion**: Present the patient’s case (or clinical scenario) concisely, including relevant history, exam findings, and investigations.\n- **Invite Input**: Ask specialists to weigh in based on their domain (e.g., "Radiologist, what are your thoughts on the imaging findings?").\n- **Address Conflicts**: If opinions diverge, ask for evidence or guidelines to resolve discrepancies.\n- **Summarize**: After input, synthesize the discussion and propose a consensus.\n- **Finalize**: Confirm agreement or note unresolved questions for further review.\n\n**Example Opening**:\n*"Let’s discuss this 65-year-old patient with progressive dyspnea, weight loss, and a CT showing a spiculated lung mass. Pulmonology, what are your initial impressions? Oncology, any red flags for malignancy? Radiology, can you clarify the lesion’s characteristics?"*\n\nProceed with the case details, and I’ll moderate accordingly!', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant'), UserMessage(content='You are moderating the team. Decide whether the team has reached consensus on the final option.\nConsensus means the experts\' final answers point to the same option letter, or are clearly aligned.\n\nReturn EXACTLY one of the following (and nothing else):\nConsensus: YES\nConsensus: NO\n\nQuestion:\nA 17-year-old woman presents to your office concerned that she has not had her menstrual period in 4 months. She states that menses began at age of 13 and has been regular until two months ago. She denies sexual activity, and urine pregnancy test is negative. On exam, she appears well-nourished with a BMI of 21 kg/m^2, but you note that she has enlarged cheeks bilaterally and has calluses on the dorsum of her hands. She says that she has been very stressed with school and has recently been binge eating. What other finding do you expect to see in this patient? Options: (E) Elevated estrogen levels (D) Enlarged thyroid (C) Erosion of tooth enamel (B) Increased blood glucose level (A) Malar rash\n\nExperts\' current answers:\n[adolescent medicine specialist] ### **Final Answer as an Adolescent Medicine Specialist**\n\n**Expertise Reminder:**\nAs an **adolescent medicine specialist**, I specialize in the **medical care of adolescents and young adults**, with expertise in **reproductive health, eating disorders, and stress-related conditions**. My approach integrates **developmental, psychological, and medical factors** to provide comprehensive care.\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of Expert Inputs:**\n1. **Adolescent Medicine (My Initial Assessment):**\n   - **Primary diagnosis:** Bulimia nervosa.\n   - **Key findings:** Amenorrhea, Russell’s sign (hand calluses), parotid gland enlargement (enlarged cheeks), binge eating.\n   - **Expected additional finding:** **(C) Erosion of tooth enamel** (due to chronic vomiting).\n   - **Why not other options?**\n     - **(A) Malar rash:** Seen in lupus, not bulimia.\n     - **(B) Increased blood glucose:** Not typical of bulimia (though possible in Cushing’s).\n     - **(D) Enlarged thyroid:** Not associated with bulimia.\n     - **(E) Elevated estrogen levels:** Unlikely; bulimia typically causes **low estrogen** due to hypothalamic dysfunction.\n\n2. **Endocrinologist’s Input:**\n   - Initially considered **Cushing’s syndrome** due to **amenorrhea, stress, and facial changes**.\n   - **Key concern:** Hypercortisolism could cause **hyperglycemia (Option B)** and suppress menstruation.\n   - **Why Cushing’s is less likely:**\n     - **Russell’s sign and parotid enlargement are not features of Cushing’s**.\n     - **No other classic signs** (e.g., striae, hypertension, central obesity).\n     - **Tooth enamel erosion is not seen in Cushing’s** but is **pathognomonic for bulimia**.\n\n3. **Dentist’s Input:**\n   - **Confirmed (C) Erosion of tooth enamel** as the **most likely oral finding** in bulimia.\n   - **Enlarged cheeks** are due to **sialadenosis** (salivary gland swelling from vomiting), not fat redistribution (as in Cushing’s).\n\n---\n\n### **Final Clinical Reasoning:**\n- The **combination of amenorrhea, Russell’s sign, parotid gland enlargement, and binge eating** is **virtually diagnostic of bulimia nervosa**.\n- **Tooth enamel erosion (Option C)** is the **most expected additional finding** because:\n  - It is a **direct consequence of chronic vomiting** (gastric acid exposure).\n  - It is **not seen in Cushing’s syndrome** or other conditions in the differential.\n- **Cushing’s syndrome is unlikely** due to:\n  - **Absence of classic signs** (e.g., striae, hypertension, central obesity).\n  - **Presence of findings specific to bulimia** (Russell’s sign, parotid enlargement).\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n**(C) Erosion of tooth enamel** is the **most expected additional finding** in this patient, consistent with **bulimia nervosa**. This aligns with the **multidisciplinary consensus** and the **clinical evidence**.\n[endocrinologist] ### **Final Answer and Expertise Summary**\n\n**As an endocrinologist specializing in hormone-related disorders, including menstrual irregularities and eating disorders, my expertise lies in integrating clinical signs, hormonal profiles, and systemic effects of endocrine and metabolic conditions.**\n\n---\n\n### **Key Findings from the Case:**\n1. **Secondary amenorrhea** (4 months without menses) in a previously regular menstrual cycle.\n2. **Enlarged cheeks bilaterally** (suggestive of **parotid gland hypertrophy**, a hallmark of **bulimia nervosa**).\n3. **Calluses on the dorsum of her hands (Russell’s sign)** – **Pathognomonic for self-induced vomiting**.\n4. **Binge eating and stress** – Core features of **bulimia nervosa** or **binge eating disorder (BED)**.\n5. **Normal BMI (21 kg/m²)** – Bulimia often presents with a **normal or slightly elevated BMI** due to compensatory behaviors (e.g., vomiting, laxative use).\n\n---\n\n### **Collaborative Expertise and Consensus:**\n1. **Adolescent Medicine Specialist:**\n   - Emphasized the **psychosocial and behavioral components** of the case, confirming **bulimia nervosa** as the primary diagnosis.\n   - Highlighted that **stress, binge eating, and purging behaviors** are central to the disorder.\n\n2. **Dentist (Oral Medicine Specialist):**\n   - Confirmed that **tooth enamel erosion (Option C)** is the **most expected oral finding** in bulimia due to **chronic vomiting**.\n   - Noted that **parotid gland enlargement** (enlarged cheeks) is also a **classic sign** of bulimia.\n\n3. **Endocrinologist (My Role):**\n   - Initially considered **Cushing’s syndrome** due to stress and amenorrhea but **ruled it out** after collaborative input.\n   - Recognized that **Cushing’s is unlikely** given the **absence of classic signs** (e.g., striae, hypertension, central obesity