al work** due to concerns about **fatigue, patient safety, and resident well-being**.\n\n2. **Moonlighting-Specific Rules:**\n   - **Internal vs. External Moonlighting:**\n     - **Internal moonlighting** (within the residency institution) **counts toward the 80-hour limit**.\n     - **External moonlighting** (like this resident’s Saturday shift) **also counts toward the 80-hour limit**.\n   - **Program Director (PD) Approval Required:**\n     - The resident **must obtain prior written approval** from the PD for any moonlighting.\n     - The PD must ensure the moonlighting **does not interfere with residency obligations** (e.g., fatigue, performance, or educational progress).\n   - **No Exceeding 80 Hours:**\n     - Even if the resident wants to work more on Sundays, **the total cannot exceed 80 hours** (including residency + moonlighting).\n\n3. **Fatigue & Patient Safety Concerns:**\n   - The ACGME **prohibits residents from working in a state of fatigue** that could compromise patient safety.\n   - Working **70 hours/week** (especially with a 10-hour shift on top of residency) **risks fatigue**, even if technically under 80 hours.\n   - The resident **cannot work longer shifts** (Option C) or **add 20 more hours** (Option E) without violating the 80-hour rule.\n\n4. **Sundays Are Not Exempt:**\n   - While the resident is free on Sundays, **any additional work (even on a "free" day) counts toward the 80-hour limit**.\n   - Option D (working more on Sundays) is **only permissible if total hours stay ≤ 80**, which they already approach.\n\n5. **Not a Complete Ban on Moonlighting (Option B is Incorrect):**\n   - The ACGME **does not outright ban moonlighting** but **strictly regulates it** to prevent overcommitment.\n   - The resident **could moonlight fewer hours** (e.g., 5 instead of 10) if approved by the PD.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion:**\n- The resident is **already at risk of overcommitment** (70/80 hours).\n- **Option A is correct** because the ACGME would requ