ther Options?**\n   - **(B) Calcitonin:** Effective for **acute hypercalcemia** but **not suitable for long-term use** due to tachyphylaxis.\n   - **(C) Furosemide:** Helps with **acute calcium excretion** but **not sufficient alone** for chronic management.\n   - **(D) IV fluids:** Useful for **acute management** but **not a long-term solution**.\n   - **(E) Teriparatide:** **Contraindicated** in hypercalcemia (it increases bone turnover and calcium release).\n\n---\n\n### **Final Recommendation:**\n**Option (A) Alendronate** is the **optimal long-term prophylactic agent** for this patient’s hypercalcemia, assuming the underlying cause is **malignancy-associated hypercalcemia (HCM)**.\n\n---\n\n### **Action Plan:**\n1. **Confirm the Diagnosis:**\n   - **PTHrP levels** (elevated in HHM).\n   - **Vitamin D metabolites (25-OH, 1,25-OH)** to rule out vitamin D toxicity.\n   - **CT imaging** (chest, abdomen, pelvis) to screen for malignancy.\n   - **SPEP/UPEP** to rule out multiple myeloma.\n\n2. **Acute Management (if needed):**\n   - **IV hydration (normal saline)** to enhance renal calcium excretion.\n   - **Zoledronic acid (4 mg IV)** for rapid control of hypercalcemia (if available).\n\n3. **Long-Term Management:**\n   - **Alendronate (70 mg weekly)** for **prophylaxis of recurrent hypercalcemia**.\n   - **Denosumab** if bisphosphonates are ineffective or contraindicated.\n   - **Treat the underlying malignancy** (chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery if applicable).\n\n---\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe **team’s consensus** supports **(A) Alendronate** as the **best long-term prophylactic option** for this patient’s hypercalcemia, with **malignancy-associated hypercalcemia (HCM) being the most likely diagnosis**.\n\n**Final Answer: (A) Alendronate**', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant')]
