ic measure**.\n   - **(E) Teriparatide** → A **PTH analog** used for **osteoporosis**, but **contraindicated in hypercalcemia** (would worsen the condition).\n\n4. **Addressing Potential Alternative Causes:**\n   - If the hypercalcemia were due to **vitamin D toxicity**, the **best long-term prophylaxis would be stopping vitamin D supplementation** (not an option here). Since this is not listed, **alendronate** is the next best choice for **persistent hypercalcemia**.\n   - If the hypercalcemia were due to **granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis)**, **steroids (prednisone)** would be the treatment of choice. However, **alendronate** is still a reasonable option if steroids are contraindicated or ineffective.\n\n5. **Next Steps in Management:**\n   - **Confirm the underlying cause** (e.g., malignancy, granulomatous disease, vitamin D toxicity) with:\n     - **PTHrP** (if elevated, suggests **HHM**).\n     - **Vitamin D levels (25-OH and 1,25-OH)** (if elevated, suggests **vitamin D toxicity or granulomatous disease**).\n     - **CT imaging** (to evaluate for malignancy or granulomatous disease).\n     - **SPEP/UPEP** (to rule out **multiple myeloma**).\n   - **Acute management** should include:\n     - **IV fluids (D)** for volume repletion and renal calcium excretion.\n     - **Bisphosphonates (e.g., zoledronic acid IV)** for rapid calcium lowering.\n     - **Calcitonin (B)** if severe hypercalcemia requires urgent treatment (but not long-term).\n   - **Long-term management** should include:\n     - **Alendronate (A)** or another bisphosphonate (e.g., zoledronic acid) to prevent recurrent hypercalcemia.\n     - **Treatment of the underlying cause** (e.g., chemotherapy for malignancy, steroids for granulomatous disease).\n\n#### **Conclusion:**\nGiven the **clinical presentation, laboratory findings, and expert consensus**, the **best long-term prophylactic agent** for this patient’s hypercalcemia is:\n\n**Final Answer: (A) Alendronate**'}

