uromas, medullated corneal nerves).\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe **most likely diagnosis is (D) Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) 2B**, given the **combination of MTC, pheochromocytoma, hyperparathyroidism, and neurological features**. **MEN 2A is less likely due to the absence of classic HPT dominance and lack of Marfanoid habitus.**\n(oncologist): Based on the clinical presentation and laboratory findings of this 45-year-old woman, the most likely diagnosis is **Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2A (MEN 2A)**.\n\n### **Key Findings Supporting MEN 2A:**\n1. **Thyroid Swelling (Diffuse Thyromegaly) + Elevated Calcitonin**\n   - MEN 2A is strongly associated with **medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC)**, which often presents with diffuse thyroid enlargement and elevated serum calcitonin levels.\n\n2. **Elevated Urinary VMA and Metanephrines + Elevated Serum Epinephrine**\n   - These findings suggest **pheochromocytoma**, a common component of MEN 2A (alongside MEN 2B and sporadic cases).\n\n3. **Hypercalcemia + Elevated Parathyroid Hormone (PTH)**\n   - MEN 2A is also associated with **primary hyperparathyroidismism (PHPT)**, which explains the hypercalcemia and elevated PTH.\n\n4. **Hyperglycemia**\n   - While not a classic feature of MEN 2A, hyperglycemia can occur due to stress (e.g., pheochromocytoma-induced catecholamine excess) or insulin resistance.\n\n5. **Anxiety, Tremors, Tachycardia, and Hypertension**\n   - These symptoms align with **pheochromocytoma** (catecholamine-secreting tumor) and thyroid hormone excess (if hyperthyroidism is present).\n\n### **Why Not the Other Options?**\n- **(A) Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) Syndrome** → Primarily associated with **renal cell carcinoma, hemangioblastomas, and pheochromocytoma**, but **not thyroid or parathyroid tumors**.\n- **(B) MEN 1** → Associated with **parathyroid hyperplasia, pancreatic tumors, and pituitary adenomas**, but **not medullary thyroid carcinoma or pheochromocytoma**.\n- **(D) MEN 2B** → Similar to MEN