                   |\n\n- The **aggressive MTC (diffuse thyromegaly + elevated calcitonin)** and **neurological features (tremors, anxiety, bone pain)** are **hallmarks of MEN 2B** and are **not typical of MEN 2A**.\n- While **hyperparathyroidism is rare in MEN 2B**, it **does not exclude the diagnosis** when the **dominant clinical picture aligns with MEN 2B**. The **bone pain and hypercalcemia** could also reflect **MTC bone metastases** or **secondary hyperparathyroidism**, which are more consistent with MEN 2B’s systemic involvement.\n\n#### **3. Genetic and Management Urgency:**\n- **MEN 2B is almost exclusively caused by the RET M918T mutation**, which is **highly penetrant and associated with aggressive MTC**. This mutation **confers a poorer prognosis** if untreated, necessitating **urgent genetic testing and prophylactic thyroidectomy**.\n- **MEN 2A mutations (e.g., codon 634) are less likely** given the **neurological features and aggressive MTC presentation**.\n- **Prophylactic thyroidectomy is critical in MEN 2B** due to the **high risk of metastatic MTC**, whereas MEN 2A-associated MTC is typically less aggressive and may allow for less urgent intervention.\n\n#### **4. Exclusion of Other Diagnoses:**\n- **(A) Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome:** No evidence of **hemangioblastomas, renal cysts, or retinal angiomas**.\n- **(B) MEN 1:** No **pituitary or pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors**; MEN 1 does not include **MTC or pheochromocytoma**.\n- **(C) MEN 2A:** Lacks the **neurological features and aggressive MTC presentation** seen in this patient.\n- **(E) Neurofibromatosis:** No **café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, or optic gliomas**; pheochromocytoma alone is not diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Recommendations:**\n1. **Urgent RET Genetic Testing:**\n   - **Prioritize testing for the RET M918T mutation (exon 16)** to confirm MEN 2B.\n   - If negative for M918T, test for **MEN 2A mutations (codons 609, 611, 618, 620, 634)**.\n\n2. **Prophylactic Thyroidectomy:**\n   - **If MEN 2B is confirmed**, proceed with **total thyroidectomy** due to the **high risk of aggressive MTC**.\n\n3. **Pheochromocytoma Management:**\n   - **Adrenal imaging (CT/MRI)** to assess for pheochromocytoma.\n   - **Preoperative alpha-blockade** to manage hypertension and catecholamine excess.\n\n4. **Parathyroid Evaluation:**\n   - **Monitor PTH and calcium levels** post-thyroidectomy, as hyperparathyroidism is less common in MEN 2B but may require intervention if persistent.\n\n5. **Monitor for MEN 2B-Specific Features:**\n   - **Examine for mucosal neuromas** (lips, tongue, eyelids) and **marfanoid habitus** to further support the diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nAfter **reviewing the multidisciplinary team’s discussion, clinical evidence, and genetic insights**, the **most likely diagnosis is (D) Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) 2B**. The **aggressive MTC presentation, neurological features, and genetic predisposition** strongly support this conclusion. **Urgent RET genetic testing and prophylactic thyroidectomy are critical** to manage the high malignancy risk associated with MEN 2B.\n\n**Final Answer:** **(D) Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) 2B**'}

