issing potential signals in exploratory research. This is essential for **advancing public health knowledge**.\n\n- **Compromise:**\n  **Option C** strikes the **best balance** by:\n  - **Acknowledging the lack of statistical significance** (critical for clinical practice).\n  - **Recognizing the OR as a potential risk** (critical for guiding future research).\n\n#### **3. Why Option C is the Most Appropriate**\n- **Avoids Overinterpretation:**\n  Unlike **Option E**, it does **not claim statistical significance**, which would be misleading.\n\n- **Preserves the Signal:**\n  Unlike **Option A**, it does **not dismiss the OR entirely**, which could lead to **missing a true association**.\n\n- **Aligns with Best Practices:**\n  - **STROBE Guidelines** recommend reporting effect estimates (OR) alongside CIs, even if not statistically significant.\n  - **Cochrane Handbook** encourages interpreting **effect magnitudes** in context, not just p-values.\n\n#### **4. Addressing the Cardiologist’s Concerns**\n- **For Clinical Practice:**\n  - **Do not use low education as a confirmed risk factor** in guidelines or patient counseling.\n  - Focus on **established risk factors** (e.g., smoking, hypertension, family history).\n\n- **For Research:**\n  - **Prioritize this finding** for further investigation in larger, well-powered studies.\n  - **Explore mechanisms** (e.g., health literacy, access to care) and **adjust for confounders** (e.g., socioeconomic status).\n\n#### **5. Why Not Other Options?**\n| **Option**               | **Why It’s Incorrect**                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|