ploratory laparotomy** (do not delay for further imaging).\n    - **Negative?** → Proceed to **CTA** (if stable).\n\n#### **2. CT Angiography (CTA) Findings (If Stable)**\n- **Key Questions:**\n  - Is there **contrast extravasation** from the **IVC**?\n  - Are there **associated injuries** (e.g., aorta, renal vessels, liver/spleen)?\n  - Is there a **retroperitoneal hematoma**?\n- **If IVC injury confirmed:**\n  - **Stable patient?** → **IR consultation for endovascular options** (e.g., stent graft, embolization).\n  - **Unstable patient?** → **Direct to OR for exploratory laparotomy**.\n\n#### **3. Definitive Management (Multidisciplinary)**\n##### **A. Endovascular Approach (IR-Led, if Stable)**\n   - **Indications:**\n     - **Active bleeding** on CTA (contrast extravasation).\n     - **Patient can tolerate temporary hypotension** (e.g., with balloon occlusion).\n   - **Techniques:**\n     - **Transfemoral access** to the IVC.\n     - **Covered stent graft** (if injury is focal and not involving major branches like renal veins).\n     - **Embolization** (e.g., coils or Amplatzer plugs) for distal IVC injuries.\n   - **Example Workflow:**\n     1. **Balloon occlusion** of the IVC (temporarily) to control bleeding.\n     2. **Angiography** to confirm source of bleeding.\n     3. **Embolization or stenting** as needed.\n     4. **Post-procedure CTA** to assess success.\n\n##### **B. Open Surgical Repair (Vascular Surgery-Led, if Unstable)**\n   - **Indications:**\n     - **Massive hemorrhage** requiring immediate control.\n     - **IVC injury with retroperitoneal hematoma**.\n     - **Associated injuries** (e.g., aorta, visceral vessels) requiring laparotomy.\n   - **Techniques:**\n     - **Midline laparotomy** (or subcostal if needed).\n     - **Direct repair** (primary suture or patch) if injury is small.\n     - **Ligation** (if repair is not feasible; **risk of venous congestion**).\n     - **Temporary shunt** (if repair is delayed).\n     - **Packing** (if blee